Review the five specification files below so each owns exactly one domain, nothing is duplicated across files, and no information is lost. Move every rule to the file that owns its domain. Do not modify `REQUIREMENT_TEMPLATE.md` or `logo.svg`.

## Ownership

- `REQUIREMENTS.md` â€” WHAT the system must do: functional and non-functional requirements, each independently testable, including externally observable validation, authorization, and failure behavior.
- `DESIGN.md` â€” the visual and frontend design language: brand and logo, color palette, typography, layout, component conventions, accessibility targets, and security-relevant user interactions and rendering behavior.
- `ARCHITECTURE.md` â€” HOW it is built: components, interfaces, data flow, trust boundaries, technology choices and rationale.
- `SECURITY.md` â€” the security posture: secure-coding rules, controls, trust-boundary enforcement, and open security decisions. All security controls are authored here.
- `AGENTS.md` â€” agent operating rules: coding conventions, enforcement rules, build/test/run commands, do/don't guidance. It defines no system behavior or architecture.

Non-owning files reference the owning file; they never restate its content (no repeated design tokens, security requirements, or architecture decisions). Tiebreak when content fits more than one domain: security controls go to `SECURITY.md`; externally observable product behavior stays in `REQUIREMENTS.md`; everything else goes to the owning file above.

## Procedure

1. **Inventory** â€” list every distinct requirement, rule, claim, and decision entry (`FR-*`, `NFR-*`, `OQ-*`, `DQ-*`, `AQ-*`, `DR-*`, `SC-*`, `OD-*`) across all five files and tag each with its owning file.
2. **Detect** â€” flag (a) duplicates, (b) contradictions, (c) low-signal content: filler, motivational text, restated-obvious points, dead links.
3. **Resolve** â€” for each duplicate, keep the copy in the owning file and replace the rest with at most a one-line cross-reference. Delete low-signal content; when unsure, keep it and list it in your final summary. Resolve contradictions using the owning specification. Correct a conflicting derivative statement when ownership is unambiguous. Otherwise, preserve the alternatives in the owning specification under its stable open-item ID (`OQ-*`, `DQ-*`, `AQ-*`, or `OD-*`). Do not choose an interpretation merely because it appears more secure. Other artifacts reference the decision ID and mirror its status; IDs are never reused.
4. **Verify** â€” re-read all five files end to end. Confirm no requirement, control, constraint, or threat mapping was dropped, every cross-reference and ID reference resolves to a real entry, and each file stands on its own.

